WEBVTT

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:06.530
These quick fingerless mitts offer
endless possibilities for being creative.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:10.720
I knit this pair about 10 times
in my mind, considering all

00:00:10.786 --> 00:00:13.450
the different variations I could use.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:17.680
Thankfully, I still have lots of yarn
and can make another pair using more

00:00:17.746 --> 00:00:20.720
of the ideas I've been considering.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:23.090
Here's what I ended up deciding.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:25.010
I knit a long piece of ribbing.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:30.090
The entire arm is in one by one ribbon,
and it fits nice and snug.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:32.970
The hand is plain stockinette.

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:36.760
I finished it with about one inch
of ribbing, and instead of messing

00:00:36.826 --> 00:00:41.730
with binding off that ribbing,
I knit a few plain rows and bound off.

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:46.010
This rolled edge saved me from a tedious
Kitchener stitch bind-off,

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:48.767
and it's a nice decorative touch.

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:54.280
If you aren't familiar with this cast-on,
it just adds a strand in the tubular

00:00:54.346 --> 00:00:56.250
edge that I can pull up.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:00.930
I gathered up this edge and hit
it with a shot of steam to set it.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:05.040
This keeps the cast
on edge from flaring out.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:08.320
Following my pattern,
I knit in ribbing to the start

00:01:08.386 --> 00:01:12.880
of the thumb opening, and then
I placed markers on each side.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:15.960
I was done with my ribbing,
so I transferred all

00:01:16.026 --> 00:01:17.850
of the stitches to the main bed.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:22.720
I knit to the end of the thumb opening
and placed another set of markers.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:27.200
Now this is designed as a fast project,
so there isn't a thumb and no

00:01:27.266 --> 00:01:30.490
messing around with the thumb gore.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:35.370
Again, following my pattern,
I finished the hand in stockinette.

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:39.167
I transferred the stitches back down
to the ribber, switched out

00:01:39.567 --> 00:01:42.720
the connecting arm, and raised my ribber.

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:48.130
I knit one inch in ribbing.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:50.880
Now, one more time,
I transferred the stitches back

00:01:50.946 --> 00:01:56.250
to the main bed and dropped the ribber,
switching the connecting arms

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:00.000
and just knit a few rows.

00:02:00.066 --> 00:02:04.610
Binding off loosely here will allow
my mitten to stretch over my fingers,

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:08.730
but the one inch of ribbing
will keep it snug.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:13.250
Seaming with this yarn that has very
little twist was a bit of a challenge.

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:15.890
It wanted to fray and break.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:21.800
I was reminded of a suggestion years ago
from a very experienced knitter that I

00:02:21.866 --> 00:02:26.410
could use a different yarn for my seam,
something that doesn't fray or break.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:32.170
Done neatly, no one will ever
know that I used a different yarn to seam.

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:35.610
With all this yarn left,
I can't wait to start another pair.

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:40.414
They're so soft and comfy and nice
and warm for this cold weather

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:41.680
here in Southern California.

